  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {t}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{2}{5}(0)^{5/2}+C \\\\ 0 & =0+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5/2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
